---
title: Mounting
order: 3
description: Mounting filesystems, fstab, bind mounts, tmpfs
tags: [disk, mount, umount, fstab, findmnt, bind, tmpfs]
---

Mounting attaches a filesystem to a directory in the VFS tree. Managed through mount/umount commands and persisted via /etc/fstab.

@text
Basic mount and umount operations.

@code bash
# Mount a device to a directory
sudo mount /dev/sda1 /mnt

# Mount with specific filesystem type
sudo mount -t ext4 /dev/sda1 /mnt

# Mount read-only
sudo mount -o ro /dev/sda1 /mnt

# Remount read-write (without unmounting)
sudo mount -o remount,rw /mnt

# Mount with specific options
sudo mount -o noatime,nodiratime /dev/sda1 /mnt

# Mount by UUID (preferred over device names)
sudo mount UUID=a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890 /mnt

# Mount by label
sudo mount LABEL=data /mnt

# Unmount by mountpoint
sudo umount /mnt

# Unmount by device
sudo umount /dev/sda1

# Force unmount (use when device is busy)
sudo umount -f /mnt

# Lazy unmount (detach now, clean up when not busy)
sudo umount -l /mnt

# See what process is using a mountpoint
sudo fuser -vm /mnt
lsof +f -- /mnt

@text
Inspect current mounts with findmnt.

@code bash
# Show all mounts in tree format
findmnt

# Show only real filesystems (no pseudo/virtual)
findmnt -t ext4,btrfs,xfs,vfat

# Find where a device is mounted
findmnt /dev/sda1

# Find what is mounted at a path
findmnt /mnt

# Show as flat list with specific columns
findmnt -l -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS

# Show only mounts from fstab
findmnt --fstab

# Check if a path is a mountpoint
findmnt --mountpoint /mnt && echo "is a mountpoint"

# Watch for mount/unmount events
findmnt --poll

@text
/etc/fstab — persistent mount configuration. Each line defines a mount.

@code bash
# fstab format:
# <device>    <mountpoint>    <type>    <options>    <dump>  <fsck-order>

# Root filesystem
UUID=xxxx-xxxx   /           ext4   defaults,noatime           0  1

# EFI system partition
UUID=ABCD-1234   /boot/efi   vfat   defaults,umask=0077        0  2

# Data partition
UUID=xxxx-xxxx   /data       ext4   defaults,noatime,nofail    0  2

# Swap partition
UUID=xxxx-xxxx   none        swap   defaults                   0  0

# Mount all entries in fstab
sudo mount -a

# Test fstab without actually mounting (findmnt --verify)
findmnt --verify

# Key options:
#   defaults    = rw,suid,dev,exec,auto,nouser,async
#   noatime     = don't update access times (performance)
#   nofail      = don't fail boot if device is missing
#   noexec      = prevent execution of binaries
#   nosuid      = ignore SUID/SGID bits
#   nodev       = ignore device files
#   ro          = read-only
#   x-systemd.automount  = mount on first access (systemd)

@text
Bind mounts — mount a directory to another location.

@code bash
# Create a bind mount
sudo mount --bind /source/dir /target/dir

# Read-only bind mount (requires remount)
sudo mount --bind /source/dir /target/dir
sudo mount -o remount,ro,bind /target/dir

# Persistent bind mount in fstab
# /source/dir   /target/dir   none   bind   0  0

# Recursive bind mount (includes sub-mounts)
sudo mount --rbind /source/dir /target/dir

@text
tmpfs — in-memory filesystem.

@code bash
# Mount a tmpfs with a size limit
sudo mount -t tmpfs -o size=2G tmpfs /mnt/ramdisk

# tmpfs in fstab
# tmpfs   /tmp   tmpfs   defaults,size=4G,noatime,mode=1777   0  0

# tmpfs for build directories (fast compilation)
sudo mount -t tmpfs -o size=8G tmpfs /home/user/build

@text
Mount loop devices and ISO images.

@code bash
# Mount an ISO image
sudo mount -o loop image.iso /mnt/iso

# Mount a disk image file
sudo mount -o loop,offset=1048576 disk.img /mnt/disk

# Create and mount a file-backed filesystem
dd if=/dev/zero of=disk.img bs=1M count=512
sudo mkfs.ext4 disk.img
sudo mount -o loop disk.img /mnt/disk

# List loop devices
losetup -l

# Set up a loop device manually
sudo losetup /dev/loop0 disk.img
sudo mount /dev/loop0 /mnt/disk
